WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:02.970
- [Narrator] Anyone who
drives a car or trucks goods

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:04.770
across the country has
been dealing with gas

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.680
and diesel prices going up
and down throughout the year.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.300
Those price jumps were largely a reaction

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.520
to Russia's invasion of
Ukraine and Western allies

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.500
subsequent sanctions on Moscow.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.080
Now more price uncertainty's on the way.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.780
That's because a European Union
ban on Russian oil imports

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.280
kicks in on December 5th.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.810
Alongside that, the Group of Seven

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.982
and some other countries have signed up

00:00:23.982 --> 00:00:26.873
to a US pushed price cap on
crude sales of $60 per barrel.

00:00:28.815 --> 00:00:29.648
Both moves are intended to
dent a key revenue stream

00:00:29.648 --> 00:00:32.430
for Russia while keeping
global oil supplies flowing.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.630
What could that mean
for prices at the pump?

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.437
Gas and diesel prices are higher
on average than a year ago,

00:00:39.437 --> 00:00:41.070
but have been trending
down in recent weeks.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:42.690
This could change if
Russia followed through

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:44.490
of its threat to stop exporting oil

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:46.693
in response to the price cap.

00:00:46.693 --> 00:00:51.600
(Vladimir Putin speaking Russian)

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.610
The Kremlin said it won't
accept the price cap

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.790
and that it was assessing the
situation, but the cap of $60

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.980
is above the current trading
price of Russian Crude.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.170
So supply cutoff may not
be in Russian President,

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.300
Vladimir Putin's, best interests.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.220
The global diesel market,
though, may be more sensitive

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.530
to market effects that
play out from the cap.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:09.000
Stores of the fuel are low in the US,

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.260
particularly on the east coast,

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.990
which is already competing
with Europe for diesel.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:14.430
A problem that could become more acute

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.590
with any loss of Russian supplies.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.270
Europe has long relied on Russian refiners

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.460
for the fuel favored in
sectors like agriculture

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:21.720
and trucking.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.480
If Russia holds back exports
or if European refiners

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.520
can't find replacements for Russian oil,

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.670
the global market price
for the fuel could rise.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:31.320
And if diesel becomes more expensive,

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.180
the cost of transporting
goods or growing produce

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.220
could be passed onto consumers.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.560
Existing sanctions have
so far had limited impact

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.690
on Russian oil production.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.270
Nonetheless, the US and
some European countries

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.060
are preparing for any
possible supply drops.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.280
Refiners, gas stations
and other buyers got ahead

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.630
of any supply shortfalls,

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.610
importing more diesel from Saudi Arabia,

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.950
the United Arab Emirates and India.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.140
US refiners are currently
well stocked with crude,

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.060
so any impact on American consumers

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.640
wouldn't be felt for some
time, according to one analyst.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.620
Treasury secretary, Janet
Yellen, said recently

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.450
that the US could draw
further from the country's

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:04.860
strategic petroleum reserve

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.610
if Russia cuts off its supplies
and raises global prices.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.590
Any immediate major
fallout from the embargo

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:11.970
and the price cap appears unlikely

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:13.950
according to some analysts.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:15.810
And the average cost of
fuel in the US has fallen

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.270
more than 30% from record
highs in the summer

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.640
and experts say this
downward trend will continue.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.440
This means there's a chance
Americans will be paying

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.240
less at the pump as they
travel home for Christmas

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.960
than they did last year,
which was around $3.85.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.300
The lower prices could
be short lived though,

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:31.860
they tend to rise on a seasonal basis

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.470
starting in mid-February
according to Gas Buddy.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.690
China relaxing its zero
Covid policy could also cause

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.580
a price spike, as could any number

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.980
of unforeseen geopolitical tensions.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.863
The uncertainty in the oil
market is far from over.

